   "newText": "The anchor aligns with the seed motif's third cell (the center/anchor position): motif starts two cells before the anchor along the motif's axis. Example demo seed `[6,3,8,8,7]` over background 1; each 2 became that five-cell motif centered with its third cell at the 2's location. Test seed `[8,0,0,3,0]` over background 9; every 6 became `[8,0,0,3,0]` with its third cell at the former 6. Some rows may contain multiple anchors, each receiving its own motif. Check grid edges before placing motif if future inputs place anchors near boundaries. The seed itself remains unchanged (including zeros), and all unrelated background cells remain background. First test submission mistakenly aligned the motif with its start roughly at the anchor; correcting alignment so the motif's third cell overlaps the anchor yielded accepted output.\n\n### Confirmed orientation generalization for task-6178aad1\nA later confirmed instance used background 3, scattered color-5 anchors, and a **vertical** 5-cell seed at column 3, rows 18–22: `[8,6,1,6,6]`. Replace every scattered 5 with this vertical motif, aligned so the third/center value `1` occupies the anchor cell (two rows above and below), preserve the original seed, and leave other cells/background unchanged. Thus, infer motif orientation from the seed: horizontal seed => copy across the anchor's row; vertical seed => copy down/up the anchor's column. Do not assume all anchors align with the same-axis placement if seed orientation differs. This specific instance was ultimately accepted with exactly this vertical-copy rule; many failed submissions were due to mistakenly treating this instance as horizontal and then manual transcription/coordinate errors. A reliable method is to record all anchor coordinates, initialize output as a copy of the input, apply each length-5 motif at offsets -2..+2 on its axis, preserve the seed cells, then validate dimensions and inspect overlapping placements/edges before submitting."
